 ## Background
-The use of polynomial and spline kernels in volume rendering and splatting evolved from the need to address the limitations of Gaussian kernels, particularly their infinite support and fixed shape. Early splatting methods relied heavily on Gaussian functions for their smoothness and ease of integration. However, the introduction of compact support kernels (like the Raised Cosine and Beta function) allowed for more efficient culling and tuning of visual properties [S1, S1, unofficial]. The Ideal Reconstruction Function was developed to specifically target visual artifacts like Mach bands by optimizing polynomial transitions [S1, unofficial]. The Dirichlet kernel approach emerged from point cloud processing libraries, offering an alternative for grid-based accumulation [S1, unofficial]. Parabolic splatting was introduced in the context of differentiable rendering (DARB-Splatting) to provide simple, differentiable alternatives to complex radial basis functions [S2]. Kernel-Reflection Sequences (KRS) apply polynomial kernel principles to hierarchical geometric modeling, particularly for natural structures like trees [S3]. Fourier-based boundary evaluation uses polynomial accumulation (Horner's method) for efficient boundary representation in radiance fields [S4]. SPH kernels, including B-splines and Wendland functions, are widely used in physics-based simulation and visualization for their smoothness and compact support [S5, unofficial].
+The use of polynomial and spline kernels in volume rendering and splatting evolved from the need to address the limitations of Gaussian kernels, particularly their infinite support and fixed shape [G]. Early splatting methods relied heavily on Gaussian functions for their smoothness and ease of integration [G]. The Ideal Reconstruction Function was developed to specifically target visual artifacts like Mach bands by optimizing polynomial transitions [G]. The Dirichlet kernel approach emerged from point cloud processing libraries, offering an alternative for grid-based accumulation [S1, unofficial]. Parabolic splatting was introduced in the context of differentiable rendering (DARB-Splatting) to provide simple, differentiable alternatives to complex radial basis functions [S2]. Kernel-Reflection Sequences (KRS) apply polynomial kernel principles to hierarchical geometric modeling, particularly for natural structures like trees [S3]. Fourier-based boundary evaluation uses polynomial accumulation (Horner's method) for efficient boundary representation in radiance fields [S4]. SPH kernels, including B-splines and Wendland functions, are widely used in physics-based simulation and visualization for their smoothness and compact support [G].
 
